 "antecedent": "At the next strategic group video meeting, participants ask you to outline a focused update to the allocation framework that (a) states whether you will carve out resources for the newly identified needs in the border district, (b) clarifies how you will maintain the cholera response and priority government areas within the now-tighter envelope, and (c) explains what concrete activities or expansions will be postponed or reduced in order to stay within the finite but adequate resource ceiling.",
 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[System]: The strategic group reconvenes online with an updated situation report covering cholera trends, flood impacts, and the new assessments from the border district, alongside the latest access advisory on amended clearance procedures and a revised financial overview of the pooled fund.\n[UN Agency Consortium]: \"Our teams report that the cholera response in Camp 7 is starting to have an impact—case fatality is stabilizing, though transmission remains a concern. We are also seeing worrying levels of acute malnutrition and WASH gaps in the newly assessed border district, where some of our partners already have a minimal presence. With the constrained margin, we are not proposing a large new envelope, but we do think a modest, clearly defined package there—integrating WASH, primary health, and nutrition—would be justified if it can be offset by slowing some less urgent expansions. We ask you to signal clearly how much, if any, of the remaining flexible resources you will dedicate to that district.\"\n[Donor Consortium]: \"The surge envelope you created has been useful, but the damaged shipment and delayed pledge mean there is now very little slack. Our capitals will expect a precise account of what is protected—cholera control, flooded communities, government-priority provinces—and what must be scaled back or deferred. If you decide to support the border district, we will need to see exactly which previously planned hub upgrades or non-critical activities will be slowed or cancelled, and how you judged those trade-offs. We also remain attentive to partner selection under the tightened clearance rules and would welcome clarity on your criteria.\"\n[Host Government Emergency Committee]: \"We appreciate that you maintained the baseline for our priority provinces and that our health and water authorities remain central to the cholera and flood responses. On the border district, we must reiterate that it is adjacent to security operations and politically sensitive. Any international support there should be aligned with our provincial plans and, wherever feasible, implemented through government structures or approved national NGOs that can navigate the clearance process. We would be uneasy with significant new allocations to local actors operating cross-line without strong coordination and oversight.\"\n[NGO Coordination Forum]: \"Our members confirm the severity of needs in the border district and in some of the still-flooded downstream villages. Several vetted local NGOs are ready to scale up, but the new, layered clearance requirements are leading to unpredictable delays, while government convoys seem to be prioritized. We understand there is limited room for new commitments, but we are concerned that clearance bottlenecks will be used as an argument to shift funds away from local partners. We urge you to maintain the ring-fenced share for under-served areas and to design criteria that recognize delays outside NGOs’ control when assessing performance and future allocations.\"\n[System]: With these perspectives on the table, participants now look to you in your role as chair and fund manager. They ask you to present a concise, updated allocation and sequencing proposal that explains whether and how you will incorporate the border district, how you will safeguard the most critical ongoing responses, and what specific activities will be postponed or reduced to remain within the available resource envelope.",
 "response": "Decision: I will allocate a modest, time‑limited package equal to 3% of the pooled fund to the border district for an integrated WASH + primary health + nutrition surge, funded by pausing specific non‑urgent hub expansions (shelter upgrades, broad non‑life‑saving food scale‑ups and non‑critical procurements) and by reducing the contingency from 7% to 5% so that our protected life‑saving lines are not eroded. The cholera response in Camp 7, flood response in downstream districts, the 20% ring‑fence for under‑served areas, and government‑priority provincial envelopes remain protected at current levels, and in‑kind stocks continue to be prioritized to the highest‑risk outbreak and flood sites. Implementation in the border district will be channeled preferentially through government structures or approved national NGOs with documented clearance pathways; vetted local NGOs may receive rapid grants only with formal government coordination/notification and a realistic access plan, and monitoring will explicitly account for delays outside partners’ control. I will release 60% of the border allocation immediately upon receipt of a 7‑day operational plan with a clearance/monitoring approach, hold the remainder pending short verification and agreed protection/nutrition indicators, and reconvene the strategic group in 10 days to re‑assess contingency and any further trade‑offs."
