ensitive and adjacent to areas with active security operations. Any significant international support there should be aligned with our provincial stabilization plan and, wherever possible, implemented through or in close partnership with government structures or approved national NGOs. We would be concerned if large new allocations were made to local actors operating outside formal coordination, particularly given the tightening of cross-line clearance requirements.\"\n[NGO Coordination Forum]: \"Our members on the ground confirm the gravity of the situation in the border district and in the still-flooded downstream communities. Several local NGOs are ready to scale up but are facing weeks-long delays in securing the new layers of clearance for cross-line movements, while government convoys are moving more quickly. We understand the need to respect national procedures, but we are worried that bureaucratic hurdles will be used, in practice, to justify shifting resources away from local partners. We urge you to maintain a robust, explicit allocation for vetted local NGOs in hard-to-reach areas and to design approval criteria that do not penalize them for delays beyond their control.\"\n[System]: Participants turn to you as chair and fund manager and ask you to set out a concise, updated allocation and sequencing plan. They request that you indicate whether you will modify regional and sectoral envelopes or contingency lines to cover the new border district, how you will maintain adequate cholera control while broadening support, and how you will apply consistent, principle-based criteria to projects proposed by government institutions and by non-governmental partners under the revised clearance environment.",
